coi selpa'i

In several places you used {lebna} where I would use {cpacu}. E.g.,
 (1) {my cu ba zi l=E8bna lo vo'a zbib=F9'u gi'e si'a co'a kl=E0ku}
 (2) {lo cm=E0lu ke tolc=EC'o n=ECnmu cu l=E8bna lo t=E0nbo lo ra n=E0zbi}
 (3) {l=E8bna lo b=E0ktu lo k=E0jna}
 (4) {ny l=E8bna lo cm=E0lu l=E0nka gi'e cl=F9gau ly lo n=E0nba}
 (5) {la .d=F2rotis. cu k=E0rgau lo l=E0nka gi'e l=E8bna pi su'o lo n=E0nba=
}

You do use {cpacu} in other places, though. E.g.,
 (6) {lo n=ECxli cu co'a cp=E0cu kl=E0ma}
 (7) {la .d=F2rotis. cu kl=E0ma te zu'e lo nu cp=E0cu la .t=F2tos.}

Clearly you have a more general interpretation of {lebna}, particularly of
lebna3, than what is indicated in the gi'uste notes, where it is said
  "x3 is possessor and not merely source, alienation is implied"
  "cf. cpacu, where [...] previous possession is not necessarily implied"

I understand it is often useful to use gismu with more general definitions,
but in this case I am confused: How do you differentiate between {cpacu}
and {lebna}?

mu'o
mi'e .asiz.

